  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure positioned overhead today will maintain\nquiet and dry weather across the central Appalachians. Shallow\nsurface inversions and strong radiational cooling early this\nmorning have allowed for the formation of river valley. This \nfog will dissipate rapidly between 7-9AM.\n\nDaytime temperatures will climb into the mid 80s across the \nlowlands and 70s in the higher terrain under light southerly \nflow, with relative humidity remaining comfortable as dewpoints \nhold in the upper 50s to lower 60s. \n\nA prefrontal trough will approach from the west late tonight \ninto Thursday morning. Convection initiating over the Midwest is\nexpected to be in a decaying state as it moves into southeast \nOhio and western West Virginia near daybreak Thursday due to \nnocturnal boundary layer stabilization. A tightening pressure \ngradient and increasing mid-level cloud cover ahead of this \nfeature should help to limit valley fog formation late tonight, \nespecially across the Middle Ohio Valley, though patchy valley \nfog may still develop in protected valleys across the southern \ncoalfields. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe primary forecast challenge will be Thursday afternoon and \nevening as an upper-level trough and associated surface cold \nfront advance toward the Ohio River. Southerly low-level flow \nwill drive moisture pooling ahead of the boundary, with \ndewpoints rising into the upper 60s to lower 70s. Robust surface\nheating working into this moist airmass will generate moderate \nto strong instability, with mixed-layer CAPE ranging from 1000 \nto 1500J/kg by Thursday afternoon. Deep-layer bulk shear of 20 \nto 30KTs and 0 to 3 km storm-relative helicity of 50 to 100 \nm2/s2 will provide sufficient kinematic support for organization\nconvection. \n\nLinear forcing along the prefrontal trough and front will favor\na predominantly multicellular storm mode, though strong\ndestabilization and modest low level turning could support \ntransient supercell structures. The atmospheric thermodynamic \nprofile features steep low-level lapse rates exceeding 7.0C/km \nand DCAPE values between 800 and 1000J/kg. This environment \nfavors damaging downburst wind gusts as the primary severe \nthreat. A secondary threat of marginally severe hail exists, \nthough wet-bulb zero heights near 11500 to 12000 ft will promote\nmelting in weaker updrafts; severe hail up to 1 inch in \ndiameter would likely require stronger cores, likely associated\nwith the aforementioned transient supercellular mode. \nConvection will initialize across the Middle Ohio Valley early \nThursday afternoon, advancing southeastward through the Metro \nValley, southern coalfields, and central mountains before \ndiminishing Thursday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAn East Coast trough will linger through Friday, sending a\nsecondary reinforcing cold front through the area. Moisture will\nbe limited with this boundary, keeping the lowlands dry, though\nterrain-forced uplift may squeeze out a few scattered showers or\nisolated thunderstorms over the Northeast Mountains on Friday\nafternoon. Behind this front, an expansive upper-level ridge\ncentered over the central United States will amplify and shift\neastward into the weekend and early next week, placing the\nforecast area under a dry northwest flow regime. Surface high\npressure will settle across the central Appalachians, ensuring\nmostly clear skies and light winds. Daytime highs will steadily\nmoderate, climbing back into the low 90s across the lowlands by\nSunday and continuing through early next week. Forecast guidance\nmaintains the ridge position over our region, keeping any ridge-\nriding convective shortwaves well to our north through at least\nthe middle of next week.",
